2. Performance Metrics
The author says there are three ways to measure performance. 

A. Elapsed Time (Batch Time)
Question:
	How long does one big job take?
Example:

Generate payroll
50,000 employees
Timer:

Start
↓

Generate Report
↓

Stop
Result:

18 seconds
This is batch performance.

Real Examples
	• Payroll generation 
	• PDF generation 
	• Video encoding 
	• Data migration 
	• ETL jobs 

B. Throughput
Question:
	How many requests can we process per second?
Example:

1000 users
↓

Server
↓

900 requests/second
Metric:

Requests Per Second (RPS)
or
Transactions Per Second (TPS)
or
Operations Per Second (OPS)

Example
Suppose:
Each request takes

200 ms
Many clients continuously send requests.
Server handles

500 requests/sec
This is throughput.

Zero Think Time
Imagine you're testing a REST server.
Client:

Send Request
↓

Receive Response
↓

Immediately send another request
No waiting.
The client is always keeping the server busy.
This measures maximum throughput.

C. Response Time
Question:
	How long does one request take?
Suppose you visit Amazon.

Open Product Page
↓

Wait
↓

Page Appears
Measured:

350 ms
That is response time.

Think Time
Real users don't constantly send requests.
Example:

Open Product
↓

Read Page
30 sec
↓

Click Next Product
That waiting period is think time.
Response-time tests include think time to mimic real user behavior.

Throughput vs Response Time
Suppose:
Server A

500 RPS
500 ms
Server B

300 RPS
300 ms
Which is better?
If your goal is to serve as many users as possible:
Server A.
If your goal is to minimize the delay each user experiences:
Server B.
It depends on the application's requirements.

